{
  "global_card_id": "overbought_and_oversold",
  "global_canonical": "Overbought and Oversold",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "Indicator Based",
  "aliases": [
    "Overbought/Oversold",
    "Overbought and Oversold Levels"
  ],
  "definition": "Overbought and oversold describe momentum extremes identified by oscillators. Overbought signals strong bullish momentum that may precede a downturn; oversold signals strong bearish momentum that may precede an upturn. Thresholds depend on the indicator used—70/30 for RSI, 80/20 for Stochastic RSI. These conditions flag potential reversals or pullbacks but never guarantee them, as price can remain extended for prolonged periods.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "SMC-Strong-Weak-Supplement (overbought_and_oversold)": "Overbought occurs when RSI exceeds 70, indicating strong bullish momentum; oversold occurs when RSI falls below 30, indicating strong bearish momentum. These conditions reflect extreme momentum but do not guarantee imminent reversal.",
    "SMC-Strong-Weak-Supplement (overbought_and_oversold_levels)": "Price overextension levels used with the Stochastic RSI indicator: overbought when the value exceeds 80, oversold when it falls below 20. These levels indicate momentum may be extreme, potentially preceding a reversal or pullback."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "With standard RSI, a value above 70 indicates overbought and below 30 indicates oversold.",
    "With Stochastic RSI, %K and %D lines rising above 80 signal overbought and falling below 20 signal oversold (note: thresholds differ from standard RSI).",
    "Sustained or extended readings in overbought/oversold zones do not confirm an imminent reversal—Stochastic RSI in particular can stay extended for prolonged periods."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Treating overbought as an automatic sell signal or oversold as an automatic buy signal.",
    "Treating overbought/oversold as automatic reversal signals when the indicator can remain in these zones for extended durations.",
    "Applying standard RSI thresholds (70/30) to the Stochastic RSI, whose correct thresholds are 80/20."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "Overbought conditions suggest a potential downturn and oversold conditions a potential upturn, but neither should be used as a standalone buy/sell signal. Both source cards stress that strong trends and momentum can persist, so counter-trend entries based solely on these levels risk losses. The RSI-based card recommends using these levels in conjunction with other tools to confirm potential reversals. The Stochastic RSI card emphasizes that the indicator frequently resides in extreme zones for prolonged periods, so readings should be treated as an alert for possible reversals or momentum shifts rather than as immediate triggers.",
  "merge_notes": "Both cards come from the same project but describe different indicators with different thresholds: standard RSI uses 70/30, while Stochastic RSI uses 80/20 and (per its card) can remain in extreme zones for extended periods. The correct threshold depends on which oscillator is applied.",
